{"duration_api_ms":106216,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"4985021b-58d9-4be6-bbac-db41ddd80774","total_cost_usd":0.2842068,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10447,"cache_read_input_tokens":74399,"output_tokens":10853,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":6260},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10447},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":8,"outputTokens":10853,"cacheReadInputTokens":74399,"cacheCreationInputTokens":10447,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.2842068,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":6260,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"safety_stops":0,"is_error":false,"num_turns":4,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I've written the condition, a proof and worked examples to `/work/answer.md`. With three winners, the Vickrey-nearest point equals the centroid (by area) of F exactly when at least one of these holds:\n\n1. **Each single-item bidder values their item at least as much as P values the whole bundle:** min(v_A, v_B, v_C) ≥ v_P. All VCG prices are zero, and both points are (v_P/3, v_P/3, v_P/3). This is the three-winner version of \"no value cap binds\".\n2. **The three single-item values are equal:** v_A = v_B = v_C.\n3. **P outbids every pair of single-item bidders:** v_P ≥ v_i + v_j for every pair.\n\nIn all other cases the two points differ.\n\n**How the proof goes:**\n- **Shape of F.** The minimum total is R = v_P. F is the set of payments that sum to v_P, with each payment between its VCG price and its value.\n- **Vickrey-nearest point.** Projecting the VCG point onto F means raising all three payments by the same amount, capping any that hit their value.\n- **Capped case.** If any cap binds, the Vickrey-nearest point lies on the edge of F, but the centroid of a polygon is always strictly inside it, so they differ.\n- **Uncapped case.** Otherwise the Vickrey-nearest point is the centre of a triangle whose corners are cut off by the value caps. The centroid matches that centre only if all three cut-off corners are the same size, which gives the three conditions above.\n\n**Example where they differ:** v_P = 10 and values (6, 5, 4). The VCG prices are (1, 0, 0) and F is a hexagon.\n- Vickrey-nearest point: (4, 3, 3).\n- Centroid: (77/18, 59/18, 22/9) ≈ (4.278, 3.278, 2.444).\n\nThe answer also has one agreeing example for each condition.\n\nI checked the condition against an exact computation of both points on 20,000 random integer cases (values 1–10, v_P below the sum of the three values), and it matched every one. The result is for the area centroid only; I didn't check the average of the polygon's vertices, which is a different point in general.","ttft_ms":1859,"type":"result","duration_ms":113015,"uuid":"d27fa587-be94-4ecb-8e8d-4be4299f154c","ttft_stream_ms":1583,"time_to_request_ms":56,"first_content_frame_ms":1583,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
